2026-07-29T03:01:27.7418748Z             },
2026-07-29T03:01:27.7451356Z             "body": "Started from a report of suspect caching against `cnn.com` with `DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds=60` and `CacheTtlHours=2`: hundreds of Redis entries dropping in the first couple of minutes. They were not all short-TTL records — the floor was being applied as a default. Investigating that surfaced three more places where a lifetime was lost or extended in transit.\n\n## Measured, from this run's integration log\n\n`cnn.com` publishes an SOA `Minimum TTL` of **86400s** (Route 53 default: Refresh 7200 / Retry 900 / Expire 1209600 / Minimum 86400). That is the RFC 2308 negative-caching TTL, and the old code was substituting the 60s floor for it — so every absent DKIM selector and probed subdomain expired 15–120× too early. Its A/MX records genuinely are ~60s, so those entries were correctly short; the reported symptom was a mix of both, which is what made it hard to call from the outside.\n\nFor contrast in the same run: `google.com` and `gmail.com` publish 60s, `example.com` 1800s.\n\nNot measured: the SOA *record's* own TTL (the SOA check prints the record's fields, not its TTL). RFC 2308 takes the lesser of that and MINIMUM, so the realistic post-fix figure for cnn.com negatives is ~900s rather than the 7200s cap. Also not measured: the blocklist zones' SOAs, which govern the ~85 DNSBL \"not listed\" answers.\n\n## The reported problem\n\n`DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds` was standing in for a TTL whenever a response carried none, and that was the dominant path rather than a corner. Only the answer section was read, so every NXDOMAIN and NODATA looked TTL-less — and negative answers are the bulk of what a validation issues: 21 blocklist zones per MX IP replying \"not listed\", 39 absent DKIM selectors, plus the SPF, DMARC, ARC and mail-survey subdomain probes. Validating a clean domain is the worst case, because nothing is listed and nothing exists.\n\nTwo changes in `DnsCacheTtl`:\n\n- **A response that published no TTL now inherits `CacheTtlHours`**, leaving the cache-wide TTL governing exactly as it does with gating off. The floor bounds TTLs we received; it is not a substitute for one.\n- **Negative answers get the TTL the zone published for them** — RFC 2308 §5, `min(SOA.MINIMUM, SOA TTL)` from the authority section. `DnsCacheSerializer` was already capturing and persisting that SOA; only the TTL policy ignored it. It is clamped by the floor and the cap like any other published TTL.\n\nWith both, \"no TTL at all\" is genuinely rare: a referral with no SOA, or a malformed response.\n\nAlso fixes two call sites that read the answer section directly and so missed the SOA — the PTR cache, and `QuerySpeculativeAsync`, which wrote straight to the cache with no entry TTL and was giving the DKIM selector and SRV probes the full `CacheTtlHours` while every gated path around them honoured the zone.\n\n## Lifetimes crossing between L1 and Redis\n\nFour paths move a value between the tiers. Three carried the lifetime with them; the read leg did not — a hit was cached locally with a fresh full `CacheTtlHours` no matter how little was left on the Redis key. With record-TTL gating on, badly: a DNS answer bounded to sixty seconds, read from Redis at fifty-nine seconds old, was then served from L1 for the full cache TTL. It did not stop there — `SetMemoryOnly` records the same expiry in the shared-cache key index, so an emptied-Redis re-warm republished the entry with *more* life than it had ever been given.\n\n`TryGetAsync` now reads the remaining lifetime alongside the value with `StringGetWithExpiry` — one round trip either way — and returns both, so the expiry cannot be dropped by a future caller. The L1 copy takes the sooner of that and a full cache TTL.\n\n**Behaviour change worth a look:** under `CacheTtlHours=0` an L2 hit now expires with the shared key instead of never. Memory not expiring is a statement about values this instance fetched, not a licence to keep a borrowed one forever.\n\nThe cap in the other direction is for restarts and config changes: Redis outlives a pod, so keys written under a longer `CacheTtlHours` survive the deploy that lowered it. Note it bounds how long a value is *held*, not how stale it may get — on expiry we re-read the L2, find the same key alive and cache it again.\n\n## Two more leaks on the same path\n\n- **`shouldPersist`-rejected values reached peers anyway.** The predicate keeps transient errors out of the disk bag and the L2 write-through, but the re-warm is a third way in and was not gated: `SetMemoryOnly` indexed every value it cached, and `WarmSharedCache` cannot tell a timeout from a real answer. It now takes a `shareable` flag, false only on the transient path.\n\n- **The speculative DNS path had no share in the L2 at all.** It wrote with `Set` (L1, bag and index but not Redis) and read with `TryGet` (L1 only), so each pod re-probed the same 39 absent selectors and no pod could see another's answer. Fixing only the write would have been pointless with an L1-only read. `Set` now performs the write-through itself rather than leaving it to the one caller inside `GetOrCreateAsync`, and `TryGetSharedAsync` is the read half for callers that cannot use `GetOrCreateAsync` — the speculative path cannot, because it must not cache its own timeouts.\n\n## CI was not running any of this\n\nThe Redis-backed tests self-skip when nothing is listening, which is right for a dev box. CI had no Redis, so they skipped there too: the last run on `main` reported 315 of 315 passed while logging `SKIPPED: no Redis` 43 times. Every shared-cache fix in this PR would have gone green whether or not it worked.\n\nThe build job now runs a `redis:7-alpine` service on 6380. `RedisTestPrerequisiteTests` keeps it that way — with `EDNSV_REQUIRE_REDIS=1`, set only on the CI test step, an unreachable Redis fails with a message naming the consequence. A dev box without Redis still skips.\n\nThis run: **0** `SKIPPED: no Redis`, core tests **315 → 333**, all green.\n\nAlso removes five private helpers in `DnsResolverService` with no callers (`SetQueryCache`, `SetPtrCache`, `SetServerQueryCache`, `TryGetPtrCache`, `TryGetServerQueryCache`). The three `Set` ones called `ProbeCache.Set` with no entry TTL — the un-gated write this PR has just finished removing from the speculative path.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach behavioural fix was checked against its own tests by reverting the change in place first:\n\n| Change | Tests that fail without it |\n|---|---|\n| Floor-not-default | `AResponseThatPublishedNoTtlInheritsTheCacheTtl` |\n| L2 hit lifetime | `AnL2HitDoesNotRestartTheClockInL1`, `WithNoConfiguredTtlAnL2HitTakesTheSharedKeysRemainingLife` |\n| Index gating | `AWarmDoesNotPublishWhatShouldPersistRejected` |\n| `Set` write-through | `APlainSetIsWrittenThroughToTheSharedTier`, `APlainSetCarriesTheEntryTtlToTheSharedTier` |\n\nThe CI-guard test was verified in all three states: unset skips, set with Redis passes, set without Redis fails.\n\n**Unchanged:** `DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds` still ships at `0`. This PR changes what the setting means when enabled; it does not enable it.",
